1
00:00:02,035 --> 00:00:03,115
ED: <i>Previously on</i> Gotham...

2
00:00:03,237 --> 00:00:04,647
Who were those people
you were speaking with?

3
00:00:04,671 --> 00:00:05,849
You keep your nose
out of my business.

4
00:00:05,873 --> 00:00:07,000
Come now, Butch. Play nice.

5
00:00:07,174 --> 00:00:09,803
If a person comes into contact
with my blood, it changes them.

6
00:00:09,977 --> 00:00:12,674
<i>My brother, if he gets in
your mind, you're doomed.</i>

7
00:00:12,846 --> 00:00:13,846
Good-bye, Mr. Gordon.

8
00:00:15,649 --> 00:00:17,447
- Ivy's missing.
- She'll turn up somewhere.

9
00:00:17,618 --> 00:00:18,677
This time she fell.

10
00:00:18,852 --> 00:00:20,753
<i>It was a long way
down into water.</i>

11
00:00:20,921 --> 00:00:22,161
BRUCE: <i>Then maybe she survived.</i>

12
00:00:22,256 --> 00:00:23,896
- MAN: <i>What's your name?</i>
- My name is Ivy.

13
00:00:24,024 --> 00:00:26,289
Barnes asked me to put you
on a 48-hour suicide watch.

14
00:00:26,460 --> 00:00:27,604
<i>I've already signed off on it.</i>

15
00:00:27,628 --> 00:00:28,771
BUTCH: He just
cost you the election.

16
00:00:28,795 --> 00:00:31,508
Give me one reason why I shouldn't
let Butch kill you where you stand.

17
00:00:31,532 --> 00:00:32,591
There's that.

18
00:00:32,766 --> 00:00:34,644
REPORTER: <i>Oswald Cobblepot
has won the mayor's office by...</i>

19
00:00:34,668 --> 00:00:36,000
I believe in you, Oswald.

20
00:00:37,004 --> 00:00:38,882
You never believed I could
win this election on my own.

21
00:00:38,906 --> 00:00:40,049
Maybe you're not
cut out for this after all.

22
00:00:40,073 --> 00:00:41,073
What?

23
00:00:41,241 --> 00:00:42,241
Jervis. Let her go now.

24
00:00:42,409 --> 00:00:44,310
No! I'll never go with you!

25
00:00:44,478 --> 00:00:45,478
No!

26
00:00:45,746 --> 00:00:47,214
(SCREAMING)

27
00:00:49,082 --> 00:00:50,914
JERVIS: <i>You killed her!</i>

28
00:00:59,259 --> 00:01:02,354
I would like to introduce
you to my chief of staff,

29
00:01:02,529 --> 00:01:04,464
Mr. Edward Nygma!

30
00:01:04,898 --> 00:01:06,457
(CROWD CHEERING)

31
00:01:07,734 --> 00:01:08,827
(CROWD CHEERING)

32
00:01:09,069 --> 00:01:10,970
MAN: (OVER PA) <i>Welcome
your new mayor of Gotham,</i>

33
00:01:11,138 --> 00:01:13,505
<i>Mayor Oswald Cobblepot.</i>

34
00:01:13,674 --> 00:01:15,336
<i>Building Gotham safe again.</i>

35
00:01:16,610 --> 00:01:20,775
<i>Welcome your new mayor of
Gotham, Mayor Oswald Cobblepot.</i>

36
00:01:21,048 --> 00:01:22,949
<i>Building Gotham safe again.</i>

37
00:01:25,886 --> 00:01:28,446
(INAUDIBLE)

38
00:01:34,761 --> 00:01:36,286
(CHEERING CONTINUES)

39
00:01:43,003 --> 00:01:44,130
MAN: You're an angel.

40
00:01:44,304 --> 00:01:46,000
Oh, thank you so
much. Thank you.

41
00:01:46,340 --> 00:01:48,673
(CAMERAS CLICKING)

42
00:01:50,944 --> 00:01:52,435
(CROWD WHOOPING)

43
00:01:53,380 --> 00:01:55,315
(CROWD CHEERING)

44
00:02:01,188 --> 00:02:02,188
Thank you.

45
00:02:06,526 --> 00:02:08,807
COBBLEPOT: <i>There's something
I've been wanting to ask you.</i>

46
00:02:09,696 --> 00:02:11,562
Am I a good boy?

47
00:02:11,732 --> 00:02:13,223
Have I made you proud?

48
00:02:18,505 --> 00:02:19,803
I hope I'm not interrupting.

49
00:02:20,340 --> 00:02:23,469
Ah! Ed, you never met my mother.

50
00:02:24,778 --> 00:02:26,007
Wasn't she beautiful?

51
00:02:28,749 --> 00:02:30,308
A fine figure of a woman.

52
00:02:31,585 --> 00:02:34,054
She was my whole world.

53
00:02:34,287 --> 00:02:36,313
The only one that was
always there for me.

54
00:02:37,891 --> 00:02:40,053
Well, I believe
the answer is yes.

55
00:02:42,863 --> 00:02:44,195
She would be proud.

56
00:02:45,132 --> 00:02:46,409
- Do you really think so?
- Oswald,

57
00:02:46,433 --> 00:02:47,958
look at everything
you've achieved.

58
00:02:49,202 --> 00:02:51,228
The people love you.

59
00:02:51,705 --> 00:02:52,968
Gangs fear you.

60
00:02:54,141 --> 00:02:57,873
And tomorrow night, the crème of
Gotham will be gathering to celebrate,

61
00:02:58,145 --> 00:02:59,374
you.

62
00:03:00,847 --> 00:03:02,873
What more could someone ask for?

63
00:03:10,557 --> 00:03:11,820
Someone to share it with.

64
00:03:17,731 --> 00:03:20,200
My mother was the
daughter of immigrants.

65
00:03:20,367 --> 00:03:22,029
A humble cook.

66
00:03:22,202 --> 00:03:23,693
We did not have much,

67
00:03:23,870 --> 00:03:27,568
but when she was by my
side, I felt loved. Protected.

68
00:03:27,908 --> 00:03:31,936
As promised, I have rid
Gotham of its villainous monsters.

69
00:03:33,447 --> 00:03:37,077
With my mother as my witness, I
vow to you from this day forward,

70
00:03:37,250 --> 00:03:42,587
every man, woman, and child
in our great city will be safe!

71
00:03:42,889 --> 00:03:43,889
(CROWD CHEERING)

72
00:03:45,092 --> 00:03:47,323
- (GUNSHOTS)
- (ALL SCREAMING)

73
00:03:49,529 --> 00:03:50,529
RED HOOD: Spread out!

74
00:03:50,997 --> 00:03:53,466
No one is safe.
Not from us. Drop it.

75
00:03:54,901 --> 00:03:55,901
Put it down!

76
00:04:00,440 --> 00:04:01,931
(SCREAMING CONTINUES)

77
00:04:02,109 --> 00:04:03,577
(GASPING)

78
00:04:15,922 --> 00:04:19,324
You will pay for this! Dearly!

79
00:04:19,493 --> 00:04:22,395
RED HOOD: Now, now,
Mr. Mayor. Don't go losing your head.

80
00:04:22,629 --> 00:04:23,629
(EXPLODES)

81
00:04:23,830 --> 00:04:25,093
Have a nice day!

82
00:04:30,203 --> 00:04:32,229
(THEME MUSIC PLAYING)

83
00:04:37,811 --> 00:04:39,302
I can't come over tonight.

84
00:04:40,714 --> 00:04:41,714
Okay.

85
00:04:41,882 --> 00:04:45,046
I mean, that'd be three nights in a
row. That'd almost make it a thing.

86
00:04:45,218 --> 00:04:46,709
Yeah. The neighbors might talk.

87
00:04:46,887 --> 00:04:49,254
Exactly. And I have a dinner.

88
00:04:50,757 --> 00:04:51,757
It's not a date.

89
00:04:53,026 --> 00:04:54,153
None of my business.

90
00:04:54,761 --> 00:04:55,990
Uh-huh.

91
00:04:57,631 --> 00:05:00,430
So, whatever
happened with Tetch?

92
00:05:01,768 --> 00:05:03,327
I told you.

93
00:05:03,503 --> 00:05:05,836
He broke his sister
out of the GCPD.

94
00:05:06,006 --> 00:05:08,532
Bullock and I chased him
down. She died. He escaped.

95
00:05:08,875 --> 00:05:11,777
'Cause I heard a rumor there's
something strange about her blood.

96
00:05:17,551 --> 00:05:18,575
Do you like this?

97
00:05:18,919 --> 00:05:20,359
I think I've made
that pretty clear.

98
00:05:20,420 --> 00:05:22,540
- Then why are you pressing?
- Because I want to know.

99
00:05:22,656 --> 00:05:24,576
And are you asking as a
reporter, or as yourself?

100
00:05:24,691 --> 00:05:26,421
- Is there a difference?
- There should be.

101
00:05:26,793 --> 00:05:29,319
When you were a cop, did you stop
being one when you came home?

102
00:05:32,165 --> 00:05:33,497
Maybe that was my problem.

103
00:05:34,801 --> 00:05:35,801
Hmm.

104
00:05:36,837 --> 00:05:38,863
The guy I'm having dinner
with is a hematologist

105
00:05:39,039 --> 00:05:41,440
in charge of analyzing
Alice Tetch's blood.

106
00:05:42,475 --> 00:05:44,444
I'm free after 10:00,
if you wanna call.

107
00:05:45,712 --> 00:05:47,977
- Thanks for the coffee.
- (DOOR OPENS)

108
00:05:57,724 --> 00:05:58,724
(KNOCKING ON DOOR)

109
00:05:58,925 --> 00:06:00,052
We found the truck.

110
00:06:01,428 --> 00:06:02,726
(GRUNTS)

111
00:06:03,730 --> 00:06:05,007
BULLOCK: They
ditched it in The Narrows.

112
00:06:05,031 --> 00:06:08,524
We've got forensics going over it,
and teams doing a 10-block sweep.

113
00:06:08,802 --> 00:06:10,065
We already found this.

114
00:06:10,871 --> 00:06:12,169
The Red Hood Gang?

115
00:06:12,372 --> 00:06:13,649
I thought those
idiots were dead.

116
00:06:13,673 --> 00:06:15,232
My guess is they're
copying the look.

117
00:06:15,408 --> 00:06:17,502
The original Red Hood
Gang were bank robbers.

118
00:06:17,811 --> 00:06:19,973
Whoever they are, they
got a beef with Penguin.

119
00:06:20,180 --> 00:06:23,309
Expand the search to 15 blocks.
Everything goes through you.

120
00:06:23,483 --> 00:06:24,576
ED: <i>You mean through me.</i>

121
00:06:30,023 --> 00:06:31,355
Wow!

122
00:06:33,193 --> 00:06:34,957
(LAUGHS)

123
00:06:37,264 --> 00:06:39,096
<i>It is good to be back.</i>

124
00:06:39,766 --> 00:06:43,464
I have missed all
of you. (CHUCKLES)

125
00:06:44,804 --> 00:06:48,366
Harvey, still a stranger to a
haircut and a shave, I see.

126
00:06:48,541 --> 00:06:50,009
What are you doing here, Nygma?

127
00:06:50,176 --> 00:06:51,804
As Mayor Cobblepot's
chief of staff,

128
00:06:51,978 --> 00:06:54,447
I'm gonna be your liaison on
the Red Hood investigation.

129
00:06:54,748 --> 00:06:56,046
I wanna see all reports.

130
00:06:56,216 --> 00:06:58,176
And I'm going to need
access to your forensic lab.

131
00:06:58,485 --> 00:07:00,852
You think I'm gonna just let
you waltz into my precinct?

132
00:07:01,955 --> 00:07:04,186
<i>You're a cop killer who
should be in the loony bin.</i>

133
00:07:04,424 --> 00:07:06,120
- I'll be damned...
- You'll be fired.

134
00:07:08,361 --> 00:07:09,989
The Mayor can't fire me.

135
00:07:10,497 --> 00:07:13,695
No, but he can appoint a
police commissioner who will.

136
00:07:15,235 --> 00:07:18,933
So, you will carry out the
Mayor's orders, without question,

137
00:07:19,105 --> 00:07:21,700
or the GCPD has a new
captain. How does that sound?

138
00:07:24,311 --> 00:07:25,836
Every dog has his day, Nygma.

139
00:07:27,113 --> 00:07:28,308
Enjoy yours.

140
00:07:32,886 --> 00:07:34,582
And where is our lord mayor?

141
00:07:35,221 --> 00:07:36,484
Hiding under his desk?

142
00:07:38,058 --> 00:07:39,617
He's meeting with constituents.

143
00:07:41,695 --> 00:07:42,993
(COBBLEPOT SNIFFLES)

144
00:07:43,263 --> 00:07:46,722
"Anything for you,
my little Cobblepot.

145
00:07:48,001 --> 00:07:49,663
"You can count on me."

146
00:07:51,471 --> 00:07:52,666
(SNIFFLES)

147
00:07:53,473 --> 00:07:55,513
Those were the words my
mother used to whisper to me

148
00:07:55,675 --> 00:07:57,143
every night
before I fell asleep.

149
00:07:58,144 --> 00:07:59,373
She was a saint.

150
00:08:00,146 --> 00:08:04,106
Yes, a saint whose only
desire was to love me.

151
00:08:05,618 --> 00:08:07,177
<i>And what was her thanks?</i>

152
00:08:07,687 --> 00:08:10,782
Not only did she take
a knife in the back,

153
00:08:12,258 --> 00:08:15,558
but even in her death, her
memory is being desecrated.

154
00:08:15,762 --> 00:08:18,664
Seriously? This is
the reason we're here?

155
00:08:19,599 --> 00:08:23,400
A little glue. Some paint.
Momma will be good as new, huh?

156
00:08:24,671 --> 00:08:25,671
- (LAUGHS)
- (GROANS)

157
00:08:25,839 --> 00:08:29,071
No! That is not the
reason you are here.

158
00:08:30,010 --> 00:08:32,445
The Red Hoods have
challenged my authority.

159
00:08:32,612 --> 00:08:34,046
They will be made an example of.

160
00:08:35,048 --> 00:08:37,449
Tonight, I am celebrating
my historic victory...

161
00:08:37,617 --> 00:08:42,317
At The Sirens. It's gonna be
fabulous. None of you are invited.

162
00:08:44,557 --> 00:08:49,018
I want them found, and their leader's head
brought to me on a spike before nightfall.

163
00:08:51,498 --> 00:08:53,899
What're you waiting
for? Find them!

164
00:08:57,804 --> 00:09:00,535
RED HOOD: All right.
Five card draw, gentlemen.

165
00:09:00,707 --> 00:09:02,699
Someone didn't ante up.

166
00:09:02,976 --> 00:09:04,672
Come on, you cheap bastards.

167
00:09:06,780 --> 00:09:08,339
(SIGHS)

168
00:09:09,149 --> 00:09:10,549
How long are we
supposed to wait?

169
00:09:10,717 --> 00:09:12,515
Long as it takes the
boss man to get here.

170
00:09:12,752 --> 00:09:13,947
You need a lookout.

171
00:09:14,287 --> 00:09:16,222
(GUNS LOADING)

172
00:09:17,257 --> 00:09:18,520
Ah, relax, fellas.

173
00:09:18,691 --> 00:09:20,557
Hey, uh, you want us
to deal you in, boss?

174
00:09:21,628 --> 00:09:23,597
We don't have time
for games, boys.

175
00:09:24,597 --> 00:09:25,997
We got work to do.

176
00:09:32,405 --> 00:09:34,840
- (KNOCKING ON DOOR)
- (DOOR OPENS)

177
00:09:37,477 --> 00:09:40,811
Hello, Jim. Is it a bad
time? I should've called.

178
00:09:41,815 --> 00:09:42,815
No.

179
00:09:44,851 --> 00:09:45,851
Please, come in.

180
00:09:47,821 --> 00:09:48,845
(CLEARS THROAT)

181
00:09:51,758 --> 00:09:52,885
(SIGHS)

182
00:09:53,093 --> 00:09:56,063
Right. Can I get you something
to drink? A glass of water?

183
00:09:56,496 --> 00:09:57,691
I'm fine.

184
00:09:58,031 --> 00:10:00,432
I heard you were back. I've
been meaning to come see you.

185
00:10:01,835 --> 00:10:02,835
Everything okay?

186
00:10:03,002 --> 00:10:04,994
Yes. Alfred and I are both well.

187
00:10:07,073 --> 00:10:08,974
I hear you're a private
investigator now.

188
00:10:09,809 --> 00:10:11,107
I'd like to hire you.

189
00:10:11,311 --> 00:10:12,311
(SIGHS)

190
00:10:12,512 --> 00:10:14,490
Bruce, if this is about
corruption at Wayne Enterprises...

191
00:10:14,514 --> 00:10:17,848
It's not. I've put my investigation
into my father's company aside.

192
00:10:18,818 --> 00:10:19,945
It's time I moved on.

193
00:10:20,120 --> 00:10:21,360
Why the sudden change of heart?

194
00:10:21,554 --> 00:10:22,749
<i>It's far from sudden.</i>

195
00:10:23,289 --> 00:10:24,809
I've given it a great
deal of thought.

196
00:10:25,425 --> 00:10:27,758
I can't live my life if
I keep looking back.

197
00:10:28,962 --> 00:10:30,863
For what it's worth,
I think that's wise.

198
00:10:31,464 --> 00:10:33,160
So, what can I do for you?

199
00:10:33,433 --> 00:10:34,958
Ivy Pepper's gone missing.

200
00:10:35,435 --> 00:10:36,994
I didn't realize
you two were close.

201
00:10:37,203 --> 00:10:39,468
We're not. She's
a friend of Selina's.

202
00:10:40,640 --> 00:10:42,268
And does Selina
know you're doing this?

203
00:10:42,442 --> 00:10:45,276
No. I don't want to get her
hopes up if you don't find anything.

204
00:10:48,014 --> 00:10:51,473
Well, we'll start with the GCPD.
See where we go from there.

205
00:10:52,485 --> 00:10:55,546
And I think it's chivalrous,
you doing this for Selina.

206
00:10:55,722 --> 00:10:57,247
How long have you
two been a thing?

207
00:10:58,124 --> 00:11:00,389
Uh, no, no, no. Selina's
a friend. That's all.

208
00:11:00,593 --> 00:11:02,789
- Mmm-hmm. Yeah.
- No. Really. She's just a friend.

209
00:11:02,962 --> 00:11:04,021
Yeah. Yeah.

210
00:11:05,899 --> 00:11:06,899
(ED WHISTLES)

211
00:11:07,066 --> 00:11:09,365
Mr. Fox, just the man
I've been wanting to see.

212
00:11:09,536 --> 00:11:13,337
Oh, joy. Mr. Nygma.
Heard you were stopping by.

213
00:11:13,573 --> 00:11:15,201
I have questions.

214
00:11:16,176 --> 00:11:17,296
Hopefully, you have answers.

215
00:11:17,443 --> 00:11:19,435
Or what? You'll
poison me with gas?

216
00:11:19,779 --> 00:11:22,214
(LAUGHS) That gas
was a sleeping agent.

217
00:11:22,715 --> 00:11:25,480
And, to be fair, I was not
in the best place in my life.

218
00:11:25,952 --> 00:11:26,952
(BLOWS)

219
00:11:28,087 --> 00:11:31,114
To be fair, if you
ever threaten my life,

220
00:11:31,291 --> 00:11:34,853
or the life of Bruce Wayne
again, I will find a way to end you.

221
00:11:35,061 --> 00:11:37,394
Mr. Fox, we both know
you're not a man of violence.

222
00:11:37,564 --> 00:11:38,623
<i>No, I'm not.</i>

223
00:11:38,965 --> 00:11:41,799
If I was, I would have laced
some piece of lab equipment,

224
00:11:41,968 --> 00:11:44,870
one which you were
sure to touch, with a toxin.

225
00:11:45,171 --> 00:11:46,332
<i>Ricin, maybe.</i>

226
00:11:46,506 --> 00:11:48,600
I'd use saxitoxin.
Harder to trace.

227
00:11:48,975 --> 00:11:50,409
But I like your style.

228
00:11:50,677 --> 00:11:51,677
You are insane.

229
00:11:51,878 --> 00:11:54,404
Was insane. I
have a certificate.

230
00:11:55,715 --> 00:11:56,876
So, the Red Hoods?

231
00:11:57,050 --> 00:11:58,245
See for yourself.

232
00:12:01,988 --> 00:12:02,988
Mmm?

233
00:12:04,824 --> 00:12:06,224
(WHIRRING)

234
00:12:07,093 --> 00:12:09,858
<i>The gang's getaway vehicle
was abandoned in The Narrows.</i>

235
00:12:10,096 --> 00:12:12,565
As you'll notice,
its carpet fibers

236
00:12:12,732 --> 00:12:17,261
has elements of dirt, oil,
and, oddly, potassium chloride.

237
00:12:18,338 --> 00:12:19,966
A halide salt.

238
00:12:22,041 --> 00:12:23,041
Fascinating.

239
00:12:25,111 --> 00:12:26,306
So, nothing on Ivy Pepper.

240
00:12:26,479 --> 00:12:28,607
But an odd report came
in a few weeks ago.

241
00:12:28,781 --> 00:12:31,012
Okay, a construction
worker claimed

242
00:12:31,184 --> 00:12:33,710
that a redhead knocked
him out, robbed him.

243
00:12:33,886 --> 00:12:35,377
And she left this.

244
00:12:35,755 --> 00:12:36,916
That's Ivy's sweater.

245
00:12:38,057 --> 00:12:39,057
How do we find this man?

246
00:12:39,225 --> 00:12:40,225
I already called.

247
00:12:40,393 --> 00:12:41,937
All right, the guy's
down south on a job.

248
00:12:41,961 --> 00:12:45,591
But he swore up and down that the
redhead who rolled him was in her 20s.

249
00:12:46,065 --> 00:12:48,830
So? He's probably covering
for being with a young girl.

250
00:12:49,636 --> 00:12:51,347
Doubtful he would come
forward in the first place.

251
00:12:51,371 --> 00:12:53,772
Yeah. Most pervs don't wanna
call attention to themselves.

252
00:12:54,807 --> 00:12:55,831
So, it wasn't Ivy.

253
00:12:56,042 --> 00:12:58,242
It seems unlikely. But why
would she have Ivy's sweater?

254
00:12:58,478 --> 00:13:00,118
- (FOOTSTEPS APPROACHING)
- Dr. Thompkins.

255
00:13:00,480 --> 00:13:03,245
Bruce. It's good to see
you again. You got taller.

256
00:13:03,416 --> 00:13:05,578
Like I said, right? The
kid's growing like a weed.

257
00:13:06,386 --> 00:13:07,547
Nice to see you, too.

258
00:13:07,954 --> 00:13:09,718
Uh, Jim, could I have a word?

259
00:13:12,792 --> 00:13:14,488
I wanted you to hear it from me.

260
00:13:14,661 --> 00:13:17,381
Um, my engagement announcement
is coming out in the <i>Gazette</i> tomorrow.

261
00:13:17,764 --> 00:13:19,824
And it will feature
Mario's name.

262
00:13:22,168 --> 00:13:23,329
I think that's customary.

263
00:13:24,203 --> 00:13:25,364
His family name.

264
00:13:25,538 --> 00:13:26,631
You mean Falcone.

265
00:13:27,707 --> 00:13:30,040
You knew? You knew
and you didn't say anything?

266
00:13:30,209 --> 00:13:32,303
Sure I did. I said
congratulations.

267
00:13:32,545 --> 00:13:33,545
Don't be cute.

268
00:13:33,713 --> 00:13:35,682
Lee. People talk.

269
00:13:37,116 --> 00:13:38,482
Was I surprised? Sure.

270
00:13:39,452 --> 00:13:42,684
But it's also none of my
business. Your life is your life.

271
00:13:42,889 --> 00:13:44,448
And I meant what I said.

272
00:13:44,691 --> 00:13:46,159
I'm very happy for you.

273
00:13:47,660 --> 00:13:49,151
So, that's it?

274
00:13:49,896 --> 00:13:51,057
That's it.

275
00:13:54,967 --> 00:13:58,495
Okay. Um, well, I
just wanted to tell you.

276
00:14:03,576 --> 00:14:04,805
(SIGHS)

277
00:14:10,049 --> 00:14:11,642
Dr. Thompkins.

278
00:14:13,820 --> 00:14:15,982
I had heard that you were back.

279
00:14:16,422 --> 00:14:17,422
I'm so glad...

280
00:14:17,757 --> 00:14:19,225
(GLASSES CLATTERING)

281
00:14:21,694 --> 00:14:23,774
- That's for Kristen.
- You shouldn't have done that.

282
00:14:23,863 --> 00:14:26,924
Or what? I'm supposed to be scared
of you because you work for Penguin?

283
00:14:27,934 --> 00:14:30,233
My fiancé's father
is Carmine Falcone.

284
00:14:30,670 --> 00:14:34,072
I so much as whisper to
him, and you disappear.

285
00:14:36,609 --> 00:14:39,204
Tell the Mayor, whatever I
can do as ME, I'm happy to help.

286
00:14:46,619 --> 00:14:48,679
It's bad enough we're
throwing Penguin's party.

287
00:14:49,055 --> 00:14:51,490
- It's good for the club.
- Now we're his errand girls?

288
00:14:51,657 --> 00:14:54,491
Sweetie, we find the
Red Hoods, he'll owe us.

289
00:14:54,727 --> 00:14:57,458
We can ask for a favor.
Say, him not killing you?

290
00:14:57,997 --> 00:15:00,517
Well, it would be nice not to
have to watch my ass all the time.

291
00:15:01,467 --> 00:15:03,766
- (MAN WHIMPERING)
- (GASPS)

292
00:15:04,237 --> 00:15:06,797
Well, well. Who do we have here?

293
00:15:07,106 --> 00:15:09,132
Guy calls himself
The Ballistic Bomber.

294
00:15:09,475 --> 00:15:12,707
My contact at The Merc saw him
buying smoke grenades last week.

295
00:15:13,079 --> 00:15:15,105
Just like the ones the Red
Hoods used on Penguin.

296
00:15:16,182 --> 00:15:17,844
Groovy. Reckon he'll talk?

297
00:15:18,284 --> 00:15:19,752
Of course.

298
00:15:20,987 --> 00:15:21,987
(WHIMPERS)

299
00:15:22,422 --> 00:15:23,822
Maybe give him a moment.

300
00:15:24,457 --> 00:15:28,326
Now, remember, kids, you tell
the press this is all Penguin's fault.

301
00:15:28,861 --> 00:15:29,861
SISTER: <i>Leave us, please!</i>

302
00:15:30,430 --> 00:15:31,673
- Good.
<i>- Leave us in peace, please!</i>

303
00:15:31,697 --> 00:15:32,697
Okay, sir.

304
00:15:32,899 --> 00:15:34,299
- Light it up.
- Have mercy.

305
00:15:34,467 --> 00:15:36,026
PRIEST: Please. Don't. No!

306
00:15:36,602 --> 00:15:39,629
Better run, Sister. Before
the Padre goes boom!

307
00:15:39,806 --> 00:15:41,172
SISTER: No! Jesus! No!

308
00:15:41,641 --> 00:15:42,904
Don't.

309
00:15:43,142 --> 00:15:45,373
PRIEST: <i>Wait, wait.
The grenade, it's slipping.</i>

310
00:15:45,711 --> 00:15:47,179
- (EXPLODES)
- (SCREAMS)

311
00:15:48,748 --> 00:15:50,546
BUTCH: <i>Seriously?
The priest?</i> (CHUCKLES)

312
00:15:50,783 --> 00:15:53,150
You should've seen it. Ka-boom!

313
00:15:53,719 --> 00:15:56,382
But you told 'em what to say?
"This is all because of Penguin"?

314
00:15:56,556 --> 00:15:58,286
Yeah, boss. But I don't get it.

315
00:15:58,458 --> 00:16:00,324
You wanna take out
Penguin, let's take him out.

316
00:16:00,560 --> 00:16:03,029
This Red Hood stuff is
fun, but it's a lot of work.

317
00:16:03,196 --> 00:16:04,789
How about you just
do what I tell you.

318
00:16:06,432 --> 00:16:09,664
Now, I got you these,

319
00:16:10,203 --> 00:16:12,104
so you'll blend in
at the party tonight.

320
00:16:13,439 --> 00:16:15,908
- Huh? Huh?
- Nice suit!

321
00:16:17,977 --> 00:16:19,411
And that's when we kill him?

322
00:16:19,579 --> 00:16:20,579
No!

323
00:16:21,013 --> 00:16:22,777
No one kills Penguin!

324
00:16:22,982 --> 00:16:25,247
TABITHA: <i>You should.
'Cause he's gonna murder you!</i>

325
00:16:25,685 --> 00:16:27,051
(GRUNTING)

326
00:16:28,955 --> 00:16:29,955
(GASPS)

327
00:16:30,156 --> 00:16:31,954
<i>Time to have a
heart to heart, Butch.</i>

328
00:16:32,825 --> 00:16:33,825
Alone.

329
00:16:40,399 --> 00:16:42,265
(INDISTINCT CONVERSATIONS)

330
00:16:44,103 --> 00:16:45,435
You don't have to buy lunch.

331
00:16:46,205 --> 00:16:49,107
I know you're a billionaire.
But I can buy you a sandwich.

332
00:16:50,743 --> 00:16:53,235
Do you miss it? Being a cop?

333
00:16:54,447 --> 00:16:55,574
Sometimes. Yeah.

334
00:16:56,516 --> 00:16:57,882
It gave a sense of mission.

335
00:16:58,618 --> 00:17:01,315
True. There's more to
life than having a mission.

336
00:17:04,390 --> 00:17:07,326
Look, I'll keep asking
around about Ivy. But, uh...

337
00:17:08,160 --> 00:17:11,858
You want my
advice, talk to Selina.

338
00:17:13,966 --> 00:17:15,229
Tell her how you feel.

339
00:17:18,037 --> 00:17:19,369
Does that actually work?

340
00:17:20,306 --> 00:17:23,367
Sometimes. It's definitely
better than being silent.

341
00:17:24,010 --> 00:17:25,808
Before you know it,
the moment passes.

342
00:17:27,113 --> 00:17:28,945
And are you taking
your own advice?

343
00:17:29,749 --> 00:17:31,029
I'm afraid that ship has sailed.

344
00:17:31,083 --> 00:17:33,362
I'm not talking about Dr. Thompkins.
I'm talking about the woman

345
00:17:33,386 --> 00:17:34,820
- you're seeing now.
- Excuse me?

346
00:17:34,987 --> 00:17:36,979
There was lipstick on a
coffee mug on your table.

347
00:17:38,491 --> 00:17:40,084
You've become
quite the detective.

348
00:17:41,794 --> 00:17:43,854
- (SIGHS)
- So, are you?

349
00:17:44,297 --> 00:17:45,765
It's complicated.

350
00:17:46,499 --> 00:17:47,694
So is Selina.

351
00:17:47,867 --> 00:17:49,130
That I believe.

352
00:17:52,905 --> 00:17:56,569
I just wish sometimes
I had a sign.

353
00:17:57,410 --> 00:17:59,572
Unfortunately, life doesn't
usually work that way.

354
00:17:59,745 --> 00:18:03,238
REPORTER: <i>We're hours away from
Mayor Cobblepot's victory celebration.</i>

355
00:18:03,749 --> 00:18:05,945
<i>But people are asking,
will the Red Hood Gang</i>

356
00:18:06,118 --> 00:18:07,450
<i>put a damper on the festivities?</i>

357
00:18:08,421 --> 00:18:10,014
Sounds like Penguin's
having a bad day.

358
00:18:10,289 --> 00:18:11,889
I'm supposed to go
to his party tonight.

359
00:18:11,958 --> 00:18:13,790
Part of my re-engagement
with being a Wayne.

360
00:18:14,260 --> 00:18:15,421
Hmm.

361
00:18:16,128 --> 00:18:17,562
Well, give the Mayor my best.

362
00:18:18,030 --> 00:18:19,760
(CHUCKLES)

363
00:18:26,672 --> 00:18:28,231
Someone is testing me, Ed.

364
00:18:28,407 --> 00:18:31,536
They're thinking, "He's mayor now.
He has to play by different rules."

365
00:18:31,711 --> 00:18:36,081
No, they'll see, when I'm
roasting their entrails over a fire.

366
00:18:36,549 --> 00:18:38,677
Perhaps I'm thinking
about this all wrong.

367
00:18:38,851 --> 00:18:41,691
Perhaps, this is not about you at
all. What if this is about the statue?

368
00:18:42,288 --> 00:18:43,449
Of course it's about me!

369
00:18:43,623 --> 00:18:45,922
- Yes, you're probably right. Oh, dear.
- (GRUNTS)

370
00:18:46,592 --> 00:18:48,959
- Wonderful.
- Oswald, take a breath.

371
00:18:49,462 --> 00:18:51,761
(GRUNTING)

372
00:18:52,365 --> 00:18:53,560
What are you doing?

373
00:18:53,833 --> 00:18:57,429
It's an old trick I learned in the lab.
Most solvents have as their base...

374
00:19:00,873 --> 00:19:03,468
I am the son of water, but if
water touches me, I die. What am I?

375
00:19:03,643 --> 00:19:04,770
Again with the riddles?

376
00:19:04,944 --> 00:19:06,864
Salt. Most people think
of it as a food additive.

377
00:19:07,013 --> 00:19:10,415
But potassium salt is
found in detergents, soaps...

378
00:19:10,583 --> 00:19:12,211
What is your point, Ed?

379
00:19:13,252 --> 00:19:14,618
I know where the Red Hoods are.

380
00:19:15,688 --> 00:19:17,418
BARBARA: I say we
give him to Penguin.

381
00:19:17,790 --> 00:19:19,452
Knowing Penguin
will gut him like a fish?

382
00:19:19,692 --> 00:19:22,560
Ladies. Come on. After
all we've been through...

383
00:19:22,728 --> 00:19:25,129
It's his life for yours. Simple.

384
00:19:26,365 --> 00:19:28,960
I don't get it. Why'd
you turn on him now?

385
00:19:29,435 --> 00:19:31,904
I wasn't gonna hurt
Penguin, honest.

386
00:19:33,305 --> 00:19:35,570
I was gonna take down
the Red Hoods myself.

387
00:19:35,741 --> 00:19:36,936
Tonight, at the party.

388
00:19:37,109 --> 00:19:38,873
All this so you
could play the hero?

389
00:19:40,680 --> 00:19:42,615
Ever since Nygma
got out of Arkham,

390
00:19:42,782 --> 00:19:44,512
Penguin treats
me like I'm nothing.

391
00:19:45,051 --> 00:19:46,883
That pencil neck's
trying to push me out.

392
00:19:47,253 --> 00:19:49,765
Let me ask you. We figured out
you're the man behind the Red Hoods.

393
00:19:49,789 --> 00:19:52,156
How long do you think it'll
take Penguin and his lapdog?

394
00:19:54,660 --> 00:19:56,720
It's your life, baby.
You make the call.

395
00:20:01,200 --> 00:20:02,429
Let me see this through.

396
00:20:03,502 --> 00:20:06,870
I get my place beside
Penguin back, I'll protect you.

397
00:20:10,076 --> 00:20:12,739
Oh, you'll do more
than that. You'll owe me.

398
00:20:13,846 --> 00:20:16,008
And one day, I'm gonna collect.

399
00:20:16,816 --> 00:20:18,682
(CELL PHONE RINGING)

400
00:20:19,452 --> 00:20:21,353
- Gilzean.
- Butch, where are you?

401
00:20:21,687 --> 00:20:24,007
The Red Hood Gang is at a
detergent factory in The Narrows.

402
00:20:24,090 --> 00:20:25,285
<i>We're on our way.</i>

403
00:20:26,092 --> 00:20:27,219
I gotta go.

404
00:20:30,663 --> 00:20:33,428
Get your things. We're done.
I'm breaking up the band.

405
00:20:34,633 --> 00:20:36,727
I'm serious. Go.
Get out of Dodge.

406
00:20:37,002 --> 00:20:39,494
Yeah, well, not for nothing,
Butch, you promised us a payday.

407
00:20:40,339 --> 00:20:41,932
We're not leaving
till we get it.

408
00:20:42,341 --> 00:20:43,604
(TIRES SCREECHING)

409
00:20:46,312 --> 00:20:48,008
(CAR DOORS SLAMMING)

410
00:20:56,155 --> 00:20:58,784
Well, guess it was gonna
happen sooner or later.

411
00:20:59,592 --> 00:21:00,592
What?

412
00:21:02,862 --> 00:21:03,862
Sorry, boys.

413
00:21:05,731 --> 00:21:06,824
(ALL GRUNTING)

414
00:21:19,845 --> 00:21:22,906
I did it, boss. I
got 'em for you.

415
00:21:25,985 --> 00:21:26,985
(GASPING)

416
00:21:29,054 --> 00:21:30,054
(CHUCKLES)

417
00:21:35,761 --> 00:21:39,391
Peace and order have once
again been restored to Gotham.

418
00:21:39,632 --> 00:21:42,261
The Red Hood Gang is no more.

419
00:21:44,170 --> 00:21:46,196
My very own man, Butch Gilzean,

420
00:21:46,372 --> 00:21:49,706
risked life and limb to
face down the bandits.

421
00:21:49,942 --> 00:21:51,035
(REPORTERS CLAMORING)

422
00:21:51,277 --> 00:21:52,973
Was your life ever
in danger, Butch?

423
00:21:54,246 --> 00:21:56,909
I can honestly say
that if I'd had hesitated,

424
00:21:57,216 --> 00:21:58,445
I'd have been a goner.

425
00:22:00,820 --> 00:22:01,844
(COBBLEPOT CHUCKLES)

426
00:22:02,087 --> 00:22:03,817
(CAMERAS CLICKING)

427
00:22:07,426 --> 00:22:09,952
All right, people, listen
up. It's happy hour.

428
00:22:10,129 --> 00:22:12,724
Finish up those reports,
and let's call it a day.

429
00:22:13,299 --> 00:22:15,632
(INDISTINCT RADIO CHATTER)

430
00:22:19,138 --> 00:22:21,573
Hey, uh, Ed.

431
00:22:22,608 --> 00:22:25,544
Even before you went
crazy, I never liked you.

432
00:22:25,711 --> 00:22:26,906
Your zipper's down.

433
00:22:27,413 --> 00:22:28,413
(SIGHS)

434
00:22:28,881 --> 00:22:32,909
Listen, Butch did us all a favor.
Red Hoods go bye-bye. Case closed.

435
00:22:33,085 --> 00:22:34,917
Look at the way the bodies fell.

436
00:22:35,087 --> 00:22:37,199
<i>It's as if they were just standing
there, waiting to be killed.</i>

437
00:22:37,223 --> 00:22:40,455
Butch comes all the way in the room.
But only one man pulled out a gun.

438
00:22:40,793 --> 00:22:42,091
I guess they weren't threatened.

439
00:22:42,561 --> 00:22:44,029
By a 300-pound gorilla?

440
00:22:44,897 --> 00:22:47,128
Whose side are you
on anyway? Hmm?

441
00:22:47,566 --> 00:22:50,035
No one's losing sleep over
a bunch of dead crooks.

442
00:22:50,202 --> 00:22:51,397
Least of all me.

443
00:22:52,838 --> 00:22:54,033
I said wrap it up!

444
00:23:01,046 --> 00:23:03,038
What have we here...

445
00:23:16,595 --> 00:23:18,086
Burning the midnight oil?

446
00:23:18,264 --> 00:23:19,823
I was just finishing up.

447
00:23:21,066 --> 00:23:23,126
I'm guessing you're
here to reprimand me.

448
00:23:23,936 --> 00:23:25,905
Reprimand you? For what?

449
00:23:27,006 --> 00:23:28,269
Punching Nygma in the jaw.

450
00:23:29,041 --> 00:23:30,339
I just couldn't help myself.

451
00:23:30,676 --> 00:23:31,871
First I'm hearing of it.

452
00:23:33,412 --> 00:23:34,539
Well done, Doctor.

453
00:23:35,581 --> 00:23:36,581
(CHUCKLES)

454
00:23:37,116 --> 00:23:41,417
Actually, I'm here to ask
about, uh, Alice Tetch's blood.

455
00:23:41,820 --> 00:23:42,820
If you've heard anything.

456
00:23:42,988 --> 00:23:46,117
As it happens, I just received the
preliminary results back from the lab.

457
00:23:46,825 --> 00:23:48,726
- Fascinating.
- What is?

458
00:23:49,128 --> 00:23:51,757
They injected samples
into a group of rats.

459
00:23:51,931 --> 00:23:53,627
After three days,
two of the rats

460
00:23:53,799 --> 00:23:55,919
were showing marked
increases in strength and stamina.

461
00:23:56,168 --> 00:23:58,069
Well, that doesn't sound so bad.

462
00:23:58,237 --> 00:24:00,757
But the study ended after one
of the rats killed all the others.

463
00:24:02,141 --> 00:24:03,507
I'm guessing that's not normal.

464
00:24:03,676 --> 00:24:06,510
It chewed through a dozen
wire cages to get at them.

465
00:24:07,346 --> 00:24:08,346
No.

466
00:24:11,984 --> 00:24:14,510
Why would one of the rats
go psycho and not the others?

467
00:24:14,820 --> 00:24:17,180
Perhaps the others hadn't had
a chance to show any symptoms.

468
00:24:17,289 --> 00:24:18,552
Or they were immune.

469
00:24:18,757 --> 00:24:22,751
<i>Sometimes people live with a disease
for years without any adverse effects.</i>

470
00:24:23,262 --> 00:24:24,890
The lab is starting
a new study now.

471
00:24:25,864 --> 00:24:28,265
Well, keep me informed.

472
00:24:30,536 --> 00:24:33,665
Oh, and, uh,
don't stay too late.

473
00:24:33,973 --> 00:24:37,102
I'm sure that fiancé of yours
would like to see you once in a while.

474
00:24:37,309 --> 00:24:41,405
And was the captain of the GCPD
invited to our new mayor's victory party?

475
00:24:42,081 --> 00:24:44,209
You know, I lost my invitation.

476
00:24:49,421 --> 00:24:50,719
(INDISTINCT CONVERSATIONS)

477
00:24:50,956 --> 00:24:52,083
I can feel it...

478
00:24:52,324 --> 00:24:53,622
(MUSIC PLAYING)

479
00:24:54,259 --> 00:24:55,386
My beautiful Oswald.

480
00:25:06,438 --> 00:25:08,907
Heard you took down the
Red Hood Gang single-handed.

481
00:25:10,275 --> 00:25:12,107
- So to speak.
- Very funny.

482
00:25:12,277 --> 00:25:14,109
Don't forget. You owe me.

483
00:25:19,752 --> 00:25:21,846
(SIGHS) I know.

484
00:25:22,254 --> 00:25:23,916
(CAMERAS CLICKING)

485
00:25:25,557 --> 00:25:26,684
How long do we have to stay?

486
00:25:26,959 --> 00:25:29,656
As long as it takes for you to be
seen in the world, Master Bruce.

487
00:25:30,596 --> 00:25:33,657
Being Bruce Wayne. And smiling.

488
00:25:35,968 --> 00:25:38,733
So this is our life now?
Fake smiles and mingling.

489
00:25:40,339 --> 00:25:41,534
I miss the old days.

490
00:25:42,741 --> 00:25:43,902
So do I.

491
00:25:49,581 --> 00:25:51,277
- It's so nice.
- Oh, excuse me.

492
00:25:51,984 --> 00:25:55,318
Bruce, Mr. Pennyworth,
I'm so pleased you made it.

493
00:25:55,521 --> 00:25:58,201
Yeah, well, you know, we're trying
to get out a bit more, Mr. Mayor.

494
00:25:58,424 --> 00:26:00,552
Oh. Oswald, please.

495
00:26:00,859 --> 00:26:03,859
We never got the chance to thank you
for saving our lives from the ex-Mayor.

496
00:26:04,263 --> 00:26:06,630
- (CHUCKLES) That was nothing.
- ALFRED: <i>Not at all.</i>

497
00:26:06,799 --> 00:26:10,031
I found it very fortuitous you turned
up when you did with that bazooka.

498
00:26:11,904 --> 00:26:13,744
I just felt a bit sorry
for Stan, the gardener,

499
00:26:13,906 --> 00:26:16,705
'cause he was picking up
bits of Galavan for weeks.

500
00:26:18,844 --> 00:26:20,210
(CHUCKLES)

501
00:26:20,913 --> 00:26:23,075
And now here you
are, the new mayor.

502
00:26:23,248 --> 00:26:25,183
Sometimes life has
a way of working out.

503
00:26:25,350 --> 00:26:26,682
Yes, that's true.

504
00:26:32,091 --> 00:26:33,252
What is it, Master Bruce?

505
00:26:33,592 --> 00:26:34,753
A sign.

506
00:26:36,528 --> 00:26:37,723
Please excuse me.

507
00:26:40,232 --> 00:26:42,352
ALFRED: All right, well,
I'll just go and wet my beak.

508
00:26:42,401 --> 00:26:44,700
Congratulations
again, Mr. Mayor.

509
00:26:47,506 --> 00:26:49,270
(MUSIC PLAYING)

510
00:26:52,611 --> 00:26:54,011
- Hey!
- Hey yourself.

511
00:26:56,215 --> 00:26:58,377
Funny, I never realized
how short you are.

512
00:26:59,918 --> 00:27:01,079
Who the hell are you?

513
00:27:02,421 --> 00:27:03,445
Not yet.

514
00:27:04,156 --> 00:27:06,682
This is too fun.
Run along, little kitty.

515
00:27:07,359 --> 00:27:08,952
Go steal some more wallets.

516
00:27:10,729 --> 00:27:11,729
Hey.

517
00:27:13,966 --> 00:27:15,093
So sweet.

518
00:27:16,635 --> 00:27:19,298
There he is, the man of the hour.
I have a surprise for you, Butch.

519
00:27:19,471 --> 00:27:20,666
Not in the mood.

520
00:27:24,910 --> 00:27:26,139
What's in the box?

521
00:27:26,445 --> 00:27:27,606
Open it.

522
00:27:28,080 --> 00:27:29,207
(EXHALES)

523
00:27:34,153 --> 00:27:36,315
Thanks, already got one.

524
00:27:36,722 --> 00:27:38,020
Oh, of course you do.

525
00:27:38,190 --> 00:27:39,434
And you certainly
wouldn't need this one,

526
00:27:39,458 --> 00:27:41,586
as it's so similar to your
very own pocket square.

527
00:27:41,760 --> 00:27:43,388
This is from the Red
Hood crime scene.

528
00:27:43,562 --> 00:27:46,293
It turns out that your suit
and the Red Hood suits

529
00:27:46,465 --> 00:27:48,559
were all made by the
same tailor, Mr. Fuji.

530
00:27:49,668 --> 00:27:50,727
That's some coincidence.

531
00:27:50,903 --> 00:27:53,737
I thought so myself, which
is why I called Mr. Fuji.

532
00:27:53,906 --> 00:27:55,746
And he said that a large
man with a metal hand,

533
00:27:55,841 --> 00:27:58,281
not many of those running
around, bought all six of the suits.

534
00:27:58,310 --> 00:28:01,212
Which means, ta-da, you're
the architect of the Red Hood...

535
00:28:03,282 --> 00:28:06,650
- (GRUNTS)
- I should snap your neck right now.

536
00:28:06,919 --> 00:28:08,251
(CHOKING)

537
00:28:08,453 --> 00:28:10,445
You haven't heard my offer.

538
00:28:13,625 --> 00:28:14,991
(EXHALES)

539
00:28:16,428 --> 00:28:17,919
We kill Penguin together.

540
00:28:18,297 --> 00:28:19,297
(CHUCKLES)

541
00:28:19,565 --> 00:28:22,034
What are you talking
about? You're his guy.

542
00:28:22,334 --> 00:28:23,645
Oh, please. You
fell for that act?

543
00:28:23,669 --> 00:28:26,400
Yes, he broke me out of
Arkham. Very appreciative.

544
00:28:26,572 --> 00:28:29,701
But I was not cut out to be number two.
I've simply been waiting for my moment.

545
00:28:29,875 --> 00:28:31,810
Which you have
graciously provided.

546
00:28:32,277 --> 00:28:36,874
How would you like to
run Gotham with me?

547
00:28:37,916 --> 00:28:39,714
(LAUGHS)

548
00:28:41,019 --> 00:28:43,488
The two of us? Working together?

549
00:28:43,655 --> 00:28:45,936
You have proven yourself far
more cunning than I imagined.

550
00:28:45,991 --> 00:28:48,927
Now, I assume this little drama of
yours was meant to climax tonight.

551
00:28:49,094 --> 00:28:53,725
So, put on the
hood, kill Penguin.

552
00:28:53,899 --> 00:28:57,358
I'll help you escape.
Tomorrow, we divide up the city.

553
00:29:00,505 --> 00:29:03,634
You'd really turn on him?
After all he did for you?

554
00:29:05,744 --> 00:29:07,679
- Mr. P.
- In a heartbeat.

555
00:29:13,785 --> 00:29:14,878
No.

556
00:29:15,487 --> 00:29:16,819
Okay.

557
00:29:17,356 --> 00:29:19,723
Then I guess it's time
for surprise number two.

558
00:29:25,264 --> 00:29:26,357
You got to Zsasz?

559
00:29:26,531 --> 00:29:27,726
I got to Zsasz.

560
00:29:29,268 --> 00:29:31,066
And he got to Tabitha.

561
00:29:32,437 --> 00:29:33,666
<i>Penguin's history.</i>

562
00:29:34,406 --> 00:29:37,308
Question is, are you?

563
00:29:51,590 --> 00:29:52,819
Can I see your invitation?

564
00:29:54,226 --> 00:29:56,752
- (SIGHS) What are you doing here?
- I was invited.

565
00:29:57,029 --> 00:29:58,657
Of course you were.

566
00:29:59,865 --> 00:30:00,958
You recognize her?

567
00:30:03,602 --> 00:30:05,264
Should I?

568
00:30:05,871 --> 00:30:07,032
I don't know.

569
00:30:09,708 --> 00:30:11,142
Hey. Are you, you?

570
00:30:11,310 --> 00:30:12,310
I'm me.

571
00:30:12,477 --> 00:30:13,570
Uh-huh. (CHUCKLES)

572
00:30:15,013 --> 00:30:16,140
Well, I'm working, so...

573
00:30:16,315 --> 00:30:18,978
Actually, there was
something I wanted to tell you.

574
00:30:19,885 --> 00:30:21,547
Could we go
somewhere more private?

575
00:30:24,189 --> 00:30:25,817
Fine. But make it quick.

576
00:30:33,832 --> 00:30:35,926
Can't believe you'd
turn on Oswald, too.

577
00:30:36,101 --> 00:30:38,536
Uh, hello. Pot-kettle-black.

578
00:30:38,704 --> 00:30:40,366
No. It's not the
same thing. You...

579
00:30:41,807 --> 00:30:42,807
Never mind.

580
00:30:43,909 --> 00:30:46,344
I swear, your goons
hurt Tabitha, so help me...

581
00:30:46,511 --> 00:30:49,310
Just do your job.

582
00:30:49,781 --> 00:30:51,272
- (APPLAUSE)
- Huh.

583
00:30:51,683 --> 00:30:53,174
Showtime.

584
00:30:54,319 --> 00:30:57,551
Put one in the Mayor
before I put one in Tabitha.

585
00:31:00,525 --> 00:31:02,460
<i>Ladies, gentlemen.</i>

586
00:31:02,994 --> 00:31:04,792
<i>Welcome to The Sirens.</i>

587
00:31:05,397 --> 00:31:09,858
<i>Tonight we're here to celebrate
the honorable Mayor Cobblepot.</i>

588
00:31:10,035 --> 00:31:14,496
<i>Our savior from the monsters,
and the captain of our fair city.</i>

589
00:31:14,873 --> 00:31:17,570
<i>And now, the Mayor would
like to say a few words.</i>

590
00:31:19,378 --> 00:31:21,778
Ed, where've you been? I've
been looking for you all evening.

591
00:31:21,980 --> 00:31:24,347
Oh, just tying up loose
ends. I just wanted to say

592
00:31:25,684 --> 00:31:26,684
good luck.

593
00:31:27,152 --> 00:31:28,745
(CHUCKLES) Okay. Thank you.

594
00:31:30,689 --> 00:31:32,453
(CROWD CHEERING)

595
00:31:43,201 --> 00:31:47,400
<i>Tonight is a celebration,
not of my victory,</i>

596
00:31:47,773 --> 00:31:49,173
<i>but of Gotham's.</i>

597
00:31:49,341 --> 00:31:50,832
<i>This is a new day!</i>

598
00:31:55,147 --> 00:31:56,672
- (GUNSHOTS)
- (GASPS)

599
00:31:57,916 --> 00:32:00,010
I wouldn't celebrate
yet, Mr. Mayor.

600
00:32:00,285 --> 00:32:01,776
Red Hoods ain't finished yet.

601
00:32:02,020 --> 00:32:04,285
- (GASPS) Ed! What are you doing?
- Wait!

602
00:32:04,656 --> 00:32:05,656
Sorry, boss.

603
00:32:06,725 --> 00:32:07,920
Butch!

604
00:32:08,326 --> 00:32:09,692
- (GRUNTS)
- (CROWD EXCLAIMING)

605
00:32:12,030 --> 00:32:13,030
(GASPS)

606
00:32:13,498 --> 00:32:15,490
- Huh?
- Oops. (LAUGHS)

607
00:32:16,001 --> 00:32:18,129
- (GUNSHOTS)
- (CROWD EXCLAIMING)

608
00:32:18,737 --> 00:32:19,932
(GROANING)

609
00:32:21,373 --> 00:32:24,832
<i>The Mayor, our mayor,</i>

610
00:32:25,210 --> 00:32:27,805
<i>vowed that all of the Red
Hoods would be destroyed.</i>

611
00:32:27,979 --> 00:32:29,914
<i>And now we have
the real leader caught.</i>

612
00:32:30,682 --> 00:32:32,708
<i>Red-handed.</i>

613
00:32:33,318 --> 00:32:35,480
(GROANING)

614
00:32:36,421 --> 00:32:38,617
You really thought I'd
give you real bullets?

615
00:32:39,157 --> 00:32:41,058
You are an idiot.

616
00:32:41,526 --> 00:32:43,222
(CROWD EXCLAIMING)

617
00:32:43,762 --> 00:32:45,754
<i>I will kill you for this!</i>

618
00:32:49,468 --> 00:32:50,988
Sounds like we're
missing all the fun.

619
00:32:51,102 --> 00:32:52,365
Got plenty of fun left for you.

620
00:32:53,605 --> 00:32:56,097
You know, if you're gonna take
a girl hostage in her own place,

621
00:32:56,274 --> 00:32:57,554
you might wanna search it first.

622
00:32:58,210 --> 00:32:59,210
(GRUNTS)

623
00:33:06,818 --> 00:33:08,616
After all that
I've done for you!

624
00:33:09,054 --> 00:33:10,113
I gave you a job!

625
00:33:10,288 --> 00:33:11,847
I gave you everything!

626
00:33:13,325 --> 00:33:18,161
I used to be somebody in this town,
and then you and that sniveling little...

627
00:33:18,396 --> 00:33:19,762
- (GRUNTS)
- (LAUGHS)

628
00:33:20,098 --> 00:33:21,259
Shut up!

629
00:33:23,201 --> 00:33:24,965
(GASPING)

630
00:33:27,739 --> 00:33:31,506
<i>I am shocked, and grieved,</i>

631
00:33:32,577 --> 00:33:35,672
<i>that one of my dearest
friends has betrayed me.</i>

632
00:33:37,315 --> 00:33:40,376
<i>But let it be known
that Oswald Cobblepot</i>

633
00:33:40,585 --> 00:33:43,783
<i>will prosecute anyone
who threatens Gotham.</i>

634
00:33:44,022 --> 00:33:45,217
Hear, hear.

635
00:33:45,657 --> 00:33:46,681
<i>- Yeah!</i>
- (CROWD CHEERING)

636
00:33:49,261 --> 00:33:51,287
- Upsy-daisy.
- (GROANING)

637
00:33:51,796 --> 00:33:53,697
- (GASPS)
- Oh, my God!

638
00:33:54,599 --> 00:33:55,658
(LAUGHS)

639
00:33:56,501 --> 00:33:57,501
Showtime.

640
00:33:57,802 --> 00:33:58,802
(GUN CLATTERS)

641
00:33:59,905 --> 00:34:00,998
(GLASS SHATTERING)

642
00:34:02,207 --> 00:34:03,266
Oswald, move!

643
00:34:03,508 --> 00:34:05,443
(GRUNTING)

644
00:34:06,378 --> 00:34:08,142
I am gonna enjoy this!

645
00:34:08,446 --> 00:34:09,446
(GASPING)

646
00:34:11,116 --> 00:34:13,449
Best party ever.

647
00:34:14,486 --> 00:34:15,486
(BOTTLE SHATTERS)

648
00:34:15,754 --> 00:34:17,950
- Wait, not now.
- COBBLEPOT: <i>Ed!</i>

649
00:34:18,290 --> 00:34:19,849
(GRUNTS)

650
00:34:20,725 --> 00:34:23,217
Ed! Ed!

651
00:34:24,396 --> 00:34:25,396
Ed!

652
00:34:25,564 --> 00:34:27,624
(COUGHING)

653
00:34:40,579 --> 00:34:41,638
So, what's up?

654
00:34:41,913 --> 00:34:44,109
I hired Jim Gordon
to help me locate Ivy.

655
00:34:45,317 --> 00:34:46,717
And?

656
00:34:46,985 --> 00:34:49,921
There was a redhead that had
her sweater. But it wasn't Ivy.

657
00:34:52,857 --> 00:34:55,202
Saying it out loud, I realize it
doesn't sound that impressive.

658
00:34:55,226 --> 00:34:56,455
No. It doesn't.

659
00:34:56,628 --> 00:34:58,268
That's really why you
brought me up here?

660
00:35:00,165 --> 00:35:01,463
(SCOFFS)

661
00:35:01,700 --> 00:35:03,430
You are the real Bruce,

662
00:35:03,902 --> 00:35:06,133
'cause nobody else
would be this boring.

663
00:35:07,272 --> 00:35:08,535
Great work, Detective.

664
00:35:09,174 --> 00:35:10,665
That's not all.

665
00:35:14,813 --> 00:35:16,907
We've been friends
a long time, Selina.

666
00:35:17,315 --> 00:35:20,080
And ever since you've known me,
I have been dedicated to one thing.

667
00:35:20,352 --> 00:35:21,547
But that's done.

668
00:35:21,886 --> 00:35:23,230
<i>I don't know what's
next. But I know...</i>

669
00:35:23,254 --> 00:35:25,723
Would you please just
say what you mean?

670
00:35:25,890 --> 00:35:26,949
I like you.

671
00:35:28,960 --> 00:35:30,588
As more than a friend.

672
00:35:36,101 --> 00:35:37,821
It would be nice if you
said something back.

673
00:35:40,672 --> 00:35:42,436
How many girls have you dated?

674
00:35:42,674 --> 00:35:44,404
- What do you mean?
- (CHUCKLES)

675
00:35:44,609 --> 00:35:47,579
It's not a trick question. How
many girls have you dated?

676
00:35:47,912 --> 00:35:50,245
- None.
- None. Hmm.

677
00:35:50,749 --> 00:35:53,150
Have you ever thought
that maybe you only like me

678
00:35:53,318 --> 00:35:55,378
because I'm, literally,
the only girl you know?

679
00:35:55,553 --> 00:35:59,046
That's not true. I feel something. And
I know you do, too. We're the same.

680
00:35:59,224 --> 00:36:00,544
In what universe
are we the same?

681
00:36:00,692 --> 00:36:02,570
- The money doesn't matter!
- Try not having it.

682
00:36:02,594 --> 00:36:03,687
It matters.

683
00:36:10,468 --> 00:36:11,868
There's something between us.

684
00:36:13,304 --> 00:36:14,567
I know there is.

685
00:36:15,473 --> 00:36:16,805
You have to see that.

686
00:36:19,477 --> 00:36:21,002
Rule one,

687
00:36:22,414 --> 00:36:24,940
don't ever tell me
what I have to do.

688
00:36:34,859 --> 00:36:36,953
So, you do have feelings for me?

689
00:36:37,829 --> 00:36:38,829
I'm confused.

690
00:36:39,531 --> 00:36:40,692
Good.

691
00:36:49,841 --> 00:36:51,605
Come on, just tell me.

692
00:36:51,776 --> 00:36:53,768
I got all dressed up,
I even put on lipstick.

693
00:36:55,113 --> 00:36:57,605
I could lose my job.
What are you offering?

694
00:36:58,083 --> 00:37:00,416
Fine. Then I'll say something.

695
00:37:00,585 --> 00:37:02,520
If I'm right, you just nod.

696
00:37:03,555 --> 00:37:06,252
You ran tests on
Alice Tetch's blood?

697
00:37:06,558 --> 00:37:07,992
You found something
in her blood.

698
00:37:08,426 --> 00:37:10,725
A virus or infection
or something.

699
00:37:11,863 --> 00:37:13,343
Do I have to go
over the rules again?

700
00:37:13,531 --> 00:37:15,500
Let's have new rules, sweetie.

701
00:37:16,868 --> 00:37:19,633
I'll tell you what we're doing
with Alice Tetch's blood,

702
00:37:20,171 --> 00:37:22,367
and in return you...

703
00:37:22,540 --> 00:37:23,633
Okay.

704
00:37:25,944 --> 00:37:28,175
Excuse me, what are you doing?

705
00:37:29,781 --> 00:37:30,805
(SIGHS)

706
00:37:31,049 --> 00:37:33,450
Two whiskeys and a
pineapple juice for this guy.

707
00:37:34,586 --> 00:37:35,826
- MAN: <i>What's going on?</i>
- To go.

708
00:37:36,154 --> 00:37:37,154
What's going on?

709
00:37:39,424 --> 00:37:40,653
I think you were just leaving.

710
00:37:44,229 --> 00:37:45,322
Forget this.

711
00:37:45,563 --> 00:37:47,088
Trust me, I'm gonna try.

712
00:37:51,169 --> 00:37:52,467
(SIGHS)

713
00:37:53,138 --> 00:37:54,197
What are you doing here?

714
00:37:54,706 --> 00:37:55,765
Just walking by.

715
00:37:55,940 --> 00:37:58,171
Saw you through the
window, took it as a sign.

716
00:37:58,343 --> 00:37:59,470
Like hell.

717
00:37:59,744 --> 00:38:01,042
You tracked me here.

718
00:38:02,213 --> 00:38:05,183
And for the record, I could've
gotten what I wanted from that creep

719
00:38:05,350 --> 00:38:06,909
without compromising my honor.

720
00:38:07,552 --> 00:38:08,713
I know.

721
00:38:09,120 --> 00:38:10,179
But you don't have to.

722
00:38:11,422 --> 00:38:12,822
I'll tell you what
you wanna know.

723
00:38:15,059 --> 00:38:17,505
I thought you wanted to keep the
woman and the reporter separate.

724
00:38:17,529 --> 00:38:19,088
- Is that an option?
- No.

725
00:38:20,932 --> 00:38:21,932
Well, then.

726
00:38:26,137 --> 00:38:28,072
You are gonna have to
buy me dinner though.

727
00:38:28,706 --> 00:38:30,106
What's good here?

728
00:38:30,475 --> 00:38:31,966
(COUGHING)

729
00:38:41,452 --> 00:38:43,045
It's ginger tea with honey.

730
00:38:43,488 --> 00:38:45,787
It's my mother's
remedy for a sore throat.

731
00:38:48,226 --> 00:38:49,694
You sure you don't
need a doctor?

732
00:38:50,195 --> 00:38:51,458
No. I'm fine.

733
00:38:51,830 --> 00:38:52,830
(COUGHS)

734
00:38:53,431 --> 00:38:56,111
I still don't understand why you
didn't tell me what you were doing.

735
00:38:58,136 --> 00:39:01,265
Your shock, when seeing
Butch, had to be genuine.

736
00:39:02,740 --> 00:39:05,005
The people had to believe it.

737
00:39:05,176 --> 00:39:08,146
And they did. And once
again, you're the city's hero.

738
00:39:08,313 --> 00:39:09,313
(CHUCKLES)

739
00:39:10,982 --> 00:39:12,507
You were almost killed.

740
00:39:13,051 --> 00:39:15,418
And you saved me. Again.

741
00:39:17,222 --> 00:39:18,417
(COUGHS)

742
00:39:20,158 --> 00:39:25,096
I hope you know, Oswald,
I would do anything for you.

743
00:39:27,565 --> 00:39:29,056
You can always count on me.

744
00:39:38,209 --> 00:39:39,209
Thank you.

745
00:39:55,360 --> 00:39:56,658
Need a hand, kitten?

746
00:39:57,228 --> 00:39:58,457
I got it.

747
00:39:59,097 --> 00:40:01,123
(INDISTINCT RADIO CHATTER)

748
00:40:05,536 --> 00:40:06,536
(GUN LOADING)

749
00:40:07,605 --> 00:40:08,868
(ENGINE REVVING)

750
00:40:14,479 --> 00:40:15,742
Hold on, Butch.

751
00:40:25,089 --> 00:40:27,991
Sir, the ambulance carrying
Butch Gilzean was just hijacked.

752
00:40:28,293 --> 00:40:31,422
Damn it! Put out a
BOLO. I'll call the Mayor.

753
00:40:44,876 --> 00:40:46,606
(BREATHING HEAVILY)

754
00:40:46,978 --> 00:40:47,978
Yes.

755
00:40:53,618 --> 00:40:54,938
WOMAN: (STAMMERS)
<i>Mister, please.</i>

756
00:40:55,153 --> 00:40:57,019
(JERVIS SHUSHING)

757
00:40:57,588 --> 00:41:00,956
<i>My dear, sweet sister.</i>

758
00:41:01,626 --> 00:41:04,994
Just look at what he did to you.

759
00:41:05,730 --> 00:41:07,426
He took you from me!

760
00:41:09,133 --> 00:41:12,626
We belonged together.

761
00:41:14,739 --> 00:41:15,900
And he killed you.

762
00:41:17,008 --> 00:41:18,874
But, mister, I'm not dead.

763
00:41:23,448 --> 00:41:25,178
If only that were true.

764
00:41:26,150 --> 00:41:28,813
(GASPING)

765
00:41:39,864 --> 00:41:43,232
Those who hurt you

766
00:41:44,335 --> 00:41:47,237
will feel my pain,

767
00:41:49,374 --> 00:41:53,368
when my sweet and
terrible vengeance

768
00:41:53,544 --> 00:41:55,604
<i>upon them rains.</i>

769
00:42:05,690 --> 00:42:07,682
(THEME MUSIC PLAYING)

770
00:42:40,758 --> 00:42:41,758
English -SDH
